17] [17592186124701 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124701 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124701 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124701 #edb/ref 1045 "The Domain of a Multivariable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124701 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124701 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124701 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124701 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "38eb3394-b723-5b5e-b0fc-8803e6b62fac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125608 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125608 #edb/ref 1061 41878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125608 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fc31f470-ef05-57c5-83e5-74d5c5660924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125608 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125609 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125609 #edb/ref 1061 41879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125609 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7be0d289-aa76-565b-b554-0640ab33fae2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125609 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125610 #edb/ref 1061 41882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7e9f458-f87e-5aa2-a10b-9d3c56318005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125611 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125611 #edb/ref 1061 41892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125611 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7cb633e3-6b78-502b-8402-00d3cc9b88b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125611 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125614 #edb/ref 1061 41906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b55f77a9-8540-5fb3-b460-fd3dba21c5e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125620 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125620 #edb/ref 1061 42402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125620 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8bf79d7f-8d55-5330-9913-9bcd94008524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125620 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125621 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125621 #edb/ref 1061 42403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125621 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "177400d3-2550-582c-ad75-57483a6bbe30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125621 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125865 #edb/ref 1064 "Another way of visualizing a two-variable function is to draw its graph as a **surface** in a three-dimensional coordinate system. \n\nFor example, consider the function\n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = x^2 +y^2, \\quad (x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the (restricted) domain is given by the unit disc \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\: : \\: x^2+y^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $f$ is a surface that consists of all the points with coordinates\n\n$$\n(x, \\, y, \\, f(x,y)), \\quad (x,y) \\in D,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3c9c7eca49a0f828e53c0775afac16335033c1a8881367e49098bf7125ee2e6.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that range of $f$ is represented by the interval $R=[0,1]$ on the vertical $z$-axis. It consists of the possible $z$-coordinates of the points on our surface." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125865 #edb/ref 1065 6920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125865 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9bf49e76-e25e-5191-ad7f-95028f749c37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125865 #edb/ref 1067 "The Graph of a Two-Variable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125868 #edb/ref 1064 "A function $f(x,y)$ of two variables is a rule that maps each pair of numbers $(x,y)\\in D$ to a unique real number. Under this notation, $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2$ is the **domain** of $f.$\n\nFor instance, consider the following two-variable function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\sin(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this function by substituting concrete pairs of numbers for $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n- Let's evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{2-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(2,\\pi)$ returned a unique real number, this means that $(2,\\pi)$ belongs to the domain of $f,$ i.e., $(2,\\pi)\\in D.$\n\n- Let's now try to evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(0,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{0-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined over the real numbers.\n\nSince $f(0,\\pi)$ did not return a unique *real* number, this means that $(0,\\pi)\\notin D.$\n\nWe can also have functions of three variables $f(x,y,z),$ functions of four variables, $f(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4),$ etc. There is no limit to the number of variables a function can have. Functions with more than one variable are called **multivariable functions.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125868 #edb/ref 1065 7034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125868 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8d127fec-12ff-5644-b095-33e9b94a52d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125868 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125869 #edb/ref 1064 "The **domain** of a multivariable function is the set of points where the function is defined. \n\nFor example, consider the function \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) =  \\sqrt{1 -x^2 -y^2}.\n$$\n\nThe function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the expression under the square root is non-negative. As a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 - x^2 - y^2 \\geq 0 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx^2 + y^2 \\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the domain of our function is a disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane, as shown on the left diagram below.\n\n![](images/86/8642db9fd933ec2f15c6712c713cfaec8fb20052066dcca3915904190d3551fa.png)\n\nEach point in this domain is uniquely mapped to a real number $z.$ Possible values of $z$ can be visualized on a separate axis (depicted by the diagram on the right). \n\nIf $(x,y)$ is allowed to run over the entire domain, the set of points on the $z$-axis where the values of $f(x,y)$ land is called the **range** of the function. For this function, the range is $z\\in [0,1].$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When discussing a function's domain, we usually refer to the so-called **natural domain**, the largest set of values for which the function is defined.\n\n- We can also *restrict* the domain of a function to a subset of the natural domain. For example, we might restrict the domain of $f$ to all values of $y$ in the upper-half plane. In this case, we'd write\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  \\sqrt{1 -x^2 -y^2}, \\quad (x,y)\\in D, \\quad D = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1, \\: y\\geq 0 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis gives a domain/range diagram that looks as follows.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab832d984232b0b3b951579e5ee2727e92c9f4bf496d4fffe82f7230cfbb14.png)\n\nIn this case, restricting the domain does not affect the range. However, in general, restricting the domain *will* affect the range." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125869 #edb/ref 1065 7035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125869 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d81a3bfc-ca0f-528c-ad48-e606581c6d6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125869 #edb/ref 1067 "The Domain and Range of a Two-Variable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133781 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186370894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1039 "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133782 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1039 "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133837 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133843 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1045 "Orthogonal Sets in Euclidean Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133990 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7dcd79bf-0e4f-5dab-b7c9-aeea97042511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134280 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134280 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134280 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134280 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186134280 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "455820f5-9352-535f-a3b2-e3fbc55031b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134280 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above, which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-5{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-5}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186134663 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134663 #edb/ref 1061 20224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134663 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "86221c16-9568-5f86-9e0e-496ed4975fdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134663 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134664 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134664 #edb/ref 1061 20225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134664 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9bda0f3-170c-5249-ae0f-9df7c5ca80fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134664 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134665 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134665 #edb/ref 1061 20226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134665 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41f3017e-1bb3-5259-a7d8-a79e20fdc3e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134665 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134759 #edb/ref 1061 26158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134759 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ccc0684-303e-58b4-854a-e95ea3983445" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134759 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134771 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134771 #edb/ref 1061 26466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134771 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e07126c3-5fe7-512e-b359-08bb40e1e823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134771 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134772 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134772 #edb/ref 1061 26850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134772 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a1b3d3e8-2043-5c48-b762-c8181abbe670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134772 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135045 #edb/ref 1064 "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is an **orthogonal set** if each pair of distinct vectors from the set is orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following set:\n\n![](images/9b/9b5afbbe850cb936b16b177e0a1b42bdd94df9bdc5dd3830aadc0de328a2b0d8.svg)\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${W}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{w}_1, \\mathbf{w}_2, \\mathbf{w}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${W}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${W}$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nIf an orthogonal set is also a basis, then we call it an **orthogonal basis**. Here, $W$ is an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ because it contains three linearly independent (you can check this) orthogonal vectors." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186135045 #edb/ref 1065 3618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135045 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dd93c4b2-6ec7-5a9c-a669-7979210393dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135045 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135090 #edb/ref 1064 "A set of vectors ![](images/96/969c7c332d8285fa0419e716cad301755b3ff1f9f074ab55798ef7c6ea09b8a1.svg) is an **orthonormal set** if it is an orthogonal set of *unit* vectors. Remember that ![](images/25/257a1924bd39970f89e8a9becbac5a5f260572a7e181dedc91ebcc4bb04b0362.svg) is a unit vector if ![](images/e5/e5ae26b69002c7beb7ee09911c2f5da8f62cd1c4ab687657a7e995d89c3f319a.svg) or equivalently, ![](images/d8/d8b5f1f6c4f3c8955cffeac4e7a4407b0d7a5e26afbf95d87e6dd71cc83c0c47.svg)\n\nAn orthonormal set that is also a basis, like the standard basis, is called an **orthonormal basis**. For instance, the standard basis ![](images/f8/f80114a44b31082d68abe17a7d45f60a6455a147a82001ef548eaed69c0c609d.svg) of ![](images/46/4607eb52aa0195587414ef27616970a6b47226a3bb476e944ac079422dc1e1b0.svg) is an orthonormal basis because the vectors are orthogonal to each other and each vector in the set is a unit vector.\n\nThere are two more things to note about orthogonal sets:\n\n- Any orthogonal set of non-zero vectors is linearly independent.\n\n- An orthogonal set in ![](images/5b/5b243567f6e2c78e286689189eb1336948b7e67d92e7c15472ca77e9fcff3bb0.svg) cannot contain more than ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) vectors. More precisely, if ![](images/59/5927da2243d5e3815e5201f00b72ebe3eae6119ba048eaf7a0505c5e1fddffbc.svg) is an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors and ![](images/6b/6b4f5fffc4d51993128d1ad6ebdbf0c543dad75a0295656991f927ed23ae3714.svg) then we must have that ![](images/15/15cac3b851d0f5feb26cd7d31c10fc21eac370c2addcdbd8634030f85bc3323b.svg)\n\n**Note:** To remember that an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors in ![](images/5b/5b243567f6e2c78e286689189eb1336948b7e67d92e7c15472ca77e9fcff3bb0.svg) cannot contain more than ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) vectors, you can use the following intuitive mnemonic. There are only ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) dimensions in ![](images/55/552bf86a483b6d2163ddc2f4ed2748c55cb24e34b4687567631f9a92db8bc158.svg) and each non-zero orthogonal vector has a ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg)-dimensional span, meaning that it \"takes up\" ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) dimension of ![](images/9c/9cc82a4b9e5c3344d1b66c539046dd27eeb1a53aadd2beba64562c3b4053e84e.svg) So, you can't \"fit\" more than ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) orthogonal vectors in ![](images/9c/9cc82a4b9e5c3344d1b66c539046dd27eeb1a53aadd2beba64562c3b4053e84e.svg)" #edb/ref 1759218604541